Dear colleagues,

This quarterly briefing summarizes the most common insider-threat and phishing patterns observed in our environment. Please review before the mandatory training deadline on January 31.

SECTION 1 — INSIDER THREAT INDICATORS

Insider threats may involve employees, contractors, or partners who misuse legitimate access. Watch for these behavioral signals:

- Unusual after-hours access to sensitive repositories
- Large downloads to personal cloud storage accounts
- Attempts to bypass DLP controls or disable logging
- Repeated failed attempts to access systems outside job scope

SECTION 2 — PHISHING RED FLAGS

Modern phishing emails often mimic internal IT tickets or HR notices. Common indicators include:

1. Urgent language demanding immediate password reset
2. Mismatch between display name and actual sender domain
3. Links that resolve to newly registered domains (< 30 days)
4. Attachments with double extensions such as report.pdf.exe

SECTION 3 — REPORTING CHECKLIST

| Step | Action | Owner |
| 1 | Do not click links or open suspicious attachments | Employee |
| 2 | Forward the message as attachment to security@company.com | Employee |
| 3 | Preserve headers for Message-ID and Received trace | SOC |
| 4 | Block sender domain if confirmed malicious | Email Admin |

SECTION 4 — REMINDERS

Remember: legitimate IT staff will never ask for your password via email. When in doubt, use the official portal or call the help desk extension listed on the intranet home page.

Multi-factor authentication remains the single most effective control against credential theft. Enable it on all work accounts by end of month.

Thank you for helping keep our data safe.

